.\" Manpage for clone.sh exclude configuration file.
.TH man 5 "30 September 2024" "1.0" "clone.sh exclude configuration file man page"
.SH NAME
exclude \- configuration file for \fBclone.sh\fR that contains files/dirs to be excluded from cloning
.SH SYNOPSIS
/etc/clone/exclude, ~/.config/clone/exclude, <pathname>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The default location of the \fBexclude\fR configuration file is \fI/etc/clone/exclude\fR. This file should never be deleted and if the default values need to be modified or new ones added it is best to create an override file.

\fBclone.sh\fR searches for an override file if not invoked under \fI/usr/local/bin/clone/\fR. The location of the override file must be under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR and the filename has to be \fIexclude\fR.

An override file can also be provided when invoking \fBclone.sh\fR with the \fB-e\fR command line option. This takes precedence over any other files.
.SH EXAMPLES
.I GENERAL USE

-/home/user/Music/* (exclude all files and directories under /home/user/Music/)

-/home/user/Music/  (exclude directory 'Music' under /home/user/)

-/home/user/Music   (exclude a file or directory named 'Music' under /home/user/)

A leading minus (-) sign specifies an exclude entry. Do not use quotes unless they are part of the filename or dirname in which case they have to be escaped, e.g. \\', \\". A trailing '/' specifies a directory to be excluded. No trailing '/' specifies a directory or file to be excluded.

.I TUPLES

If you want to exclude everything under a specific directory except certain files or directories you can use an exclude/include tuple. For example,

-/home/user/*               (exclude all files and directories under /home/user/)

+/home/user/business/report (include a file or directory named 'report' under /home/user/business/)

+/home/user/business/sales/ (include a directory named 'sales' under /home/user/business/)

A leading plus (+) sign specifies an include entry.

A tuple requires at least two entries and one of them has to be an exclude. If there's no exclude entry for an include entry the include entry is ignored. The exclude entry need not preceed the include entries.

In case 'report' is under a directory and you are unsure if it's a file or directory and want to exclude it only if it's a file, you can create a tuple as follows:

-/home/user/report  (exclude a file or directory named 'report')

+/home/user/report/ (include directory 'report')

Another example of a tuple:

-/home/user/a*      (exclude all 'a*' under '/home/user/')

-/home/user/c*      (exclude all 'c*' under '/home/user/')

+/home/user/[ac]z   (include 'az' and 'cz' under '/home/user/')

.I SPECIAL & ESCAPED CHARACTERS

If a filename or dirname includes spaces or parentheses or escaped characters like a newline they have to be escaped. For example,

[prompt]$ ls -1

\'/home/user/monthly\'$\'\\n\'\'reports/november\'

\'/home/user/sales/november (sorted)\'

The above filenames contain a newline ($\'\\n\'), a space and parentheses. The correct exclude entries, removing unnecessary single quotes, would be

-/home/user/monthly$\'\\n\'reports/november (escaped character syntax: $'\\<ec>')

-/home/user/sales/november\\ \\(sorted\\)   (space and parentheses escaped)

All escaped characters have to be specified as $'\\<ec>'.

.I VALID ENTRIES

.B a)\fR blank lines

.B b)\fR comments, a line starting with '#' is a comment.

.B c)\fR -|+<fspec>

A leading sign ('-' or '+') followed by a mandatory file specification (<fspec>) and an optional '/'. <fspec> specifies full pathnames of files or directories and all metacharacters of the bash shell can be used. <fspec> must have a leading '/' and if followed by one a directory is specifed. If not followed by one a directory or file is specified.
  
.I examples

-/dir1/dir2/report*  (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report', under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2/report*/ (exclude directories beginning with 'report', under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I exclude/include tuple example

-/dir1/dir2/          (exclude 'dir1/dir2' under root directory)

+/dir1/dir2/file1.txt (include 'file1.txt' under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2/file2.txt (include 'file2.txt' under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I other examples

-/dir1/dir2/????.txt

-/dir1/dir2/foot????.doc

-/dir1/dir2/best.???

-/dir1/dir2/*.pl

-/dir1/dir2/a*.*

-/dir1/dir2/[p-s]*             (files/dirs with first char in 'p-s' range)

-/dir1/dir2/[^p-s]*            (files/dirs with first char NOT in 'p-s' range)

-/dir1/dir2/[^p]*              (files/dirs with first char NOT 'p')

-/dir1/dir2/[1-5]*             (files/dirs with first char in '1-5' range)

-/dir1/dir2/{?????.sh,*st.txt} (files that match '?????.sh' AND '*st.txt')

-/dir1/dir2/*{.jpg,.mp3}       (files that have 'jpg' AND 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/?(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (0 or 1 file that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/*(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (0 or more files that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/+(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (1 or more files that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/!(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (files that do NOT have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

.B d)\fR -|+/[<path>] [<path>]<name>

A leading sign ('-' or '+') followed by a '/' and a <path> which is optional and can have a trailing '/'. All metacharacters of the bash shell can be used in <path>. The next token contains an optional <path> which can't have a leading '/'. <name> specifies filenames or dirnames. <path> and <name> must be separated by a '/'. The following metacharacters can be used in 
[<path>]<name>: *, ?, [], [^]. <name> can have a trailing '/' which specifies directories only.
  
.I examples

-/dir1/dir2/ report*      (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report' that are under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2 report*/      (exclude directories beginning with 'report' that are under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2/ dir3/report* (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report' that are under any 'dir3' directory that is under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2 dir3/report*/ (exclude directories beginning with 'report' that are under any 'dir3' directory that is under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I exclude/include tuple example

-/dir1/dir2 dir3/dir4/           (exclude directory 'dir4' that is under any 'dir3' directory that is under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2 dir3/dir4/file1.txt  (include 'file1.txt' that is under any '/dir3/dir4/' directory that is under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2/ dir3/dir4/file2.txt (include 'file2.txt' that is under any '/dir3/dir4/' directory that is under: '/dir1/dir2/' or any directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I other examples

-/dir1/dir2/  ????.txt

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/????.txt

-/dir1/di*r2/ foot????.doc

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/foot????.doc

-/dir1/dir2/  [p-s]*

-/dir1/dir2/  [p-s]*/

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/[p-s]*

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/[p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p-s]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p-s]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [1-5]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [1-5]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[1-5]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[1-5]*/

-/dir1/{?????sales,*reports}/ dir2/dir3/[^p-s]*

-/dir1/*{jpg,mp3}             dir2/dir3/[^p-s]*/

-/dir1/?(*jpg|*mp3)/          dir2/dir3/[^1-5]*

-/dir1/*(*jpg|*mp3)           dir2/dir3/[^1-5]*/

-/dir1/+(*jpg|*mp3)/          dir2/dir3/[^p]*

-/dir1/!(*jpg|*mp3)           dir2/dir3/[^p]*/

.I INVALID ENTRIES

The following examples are invalid, as the second token contains some metacharacters that are interpreted as plain characters (valid metacharacters for the second token are *, ?, [], [^]).

-/dir1/di*r2/ +(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: + ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  !(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ! ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2/ ?(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  *(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  *{.jpg,.mp3}      , interpreted as plain characters: { , }

-/dir1/di*r2/ {?????.sh,*st.txt}, interpreted as plain characters: { , }
.SH STANDARDS
The system files excluded from cloning are based on the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBclone\fR(1), \fBrsync\fR(1)
